{"id":"below-the-line-departments","title":"Below the line, department by department","question":"Which departments keep most of their contract dollars below the Board’s line, and where did that share move?","finding":"Across 34 departments with at least 20 contracts and $25 million agreed since 2019 (construction left out), the most money below the line sits with City Administrator ($2.94 billion in 3,789 contracts, 49% of its dollars); Public Health ($2.89 billion in 1,098 contracts, 32% of its dollars); Homelessness Services ($2.39 billion in 496 contracts, 51% of its dollars); Public Utilities Commission ($1.75 billion in 1,032 contracts, 21% of its dollars). 11 departments have nothing above the line at all. Comparing contracts that began in 2019 to 2021 with those that began in 2023 to 2025, the below-the-line share rose by more than five points in 8 departments and fell by more than five in 5; the largest rises: Adult Probation (55% to 100%); Children, Youth & Families (33% to 66%); Public Health (19% to 51%).","how":"Contracts not typed as construction whose term began in 2019 to 2026, grouped by the department on the contract. Below the line means under $10 million with a term of ten years or less. The two period columns give the below-the-line share of agreed dollars for contracts beginning in 2019 to 2021 and in 2023 to 2025, shown only where at least $5 million began in the period. “Within 10% under the line” counts contracts of $9 million to $10 million. Ranked by dollars below the line.","limits":"A department that buys many small things will sit almost wholly below any line; a share near 100% describes the size of its contracts, not its conduct. Several departments answer to a commission of their own that approves contracts the Board never sees; the Airport, Port, Public Utilities and Health commissions are named in the purchasing rules and audits on file. A falling share can mean one very large contract began in the later period. Amounts are today’s, after amendments. What would settle a row: the department’s own approval log for the contracts counted.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"contracts","t":"Contracts since 2019","type":"int"},{"k":"agreed","t":"Agreed amount","type":"usd"},{"k":"belowCount","t":"Below the line","type":"int"},{"k":"belowAgreed","t":"Dollars below the line","type":"usd"},{"k":"belowShare","t":"Share of its dollars","type":"pct"},{"k":"early","t":"Share, began 2019–2021","type":"pct"},{"k":"late","t":"Share, began 2023–2025","type":"pct"},{"k":"near","t":"Within 10% under the line","type":"int"},{"k":"largestBelow","t":"Largest contract below the line","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"department":"City Administrator","contracts":3874,"agreed":5955859974,"belowCount":3789,"belowAgreed":2941433903,"belowShare":0.4939,"early":0.4868,"late":0.4658,"near":103,"largestBelow":9999999},{"department":"Public Health","contracts":1155,"agreed":9056040163,"belowCount":1098,"belowAgreed":2888905106,"belowShare":0.319,"early":0.1921,"late":0.5095,"near":84,"largestBelow":9999999},{"department":"Homelessness Services","contracts":568,"agreed":4643268700,"belowCount":496,"belowAgreed":2390886611,"belowShare":0.5149,"early":0.4659,"late":0.603,"near":95,"largestBelow":9999999},{"department":"Public Utilities Commission","contracts":1137,"agreed":8507367562,"belowCount":1032,"belowAgreed":1750276694,"belowShare":0.2057,"early":0.2607,"late":0.2812,"near":25,"largestBelow":9874789},{"department":"Mayor","contracts":4502,"agreed":4106526277,"belowCount":4219,"belowAgreed":1598354319,"belowShare":0.3892,"early":0.3666,"late":0.3982,"near":14,"largestBelow":9800000},{"department":"Human Services Agency","contracts":1153,"agreed":4878643061,"belowCount":1120,"belowAgreed":1544797080,"belowShare":0.3166,"early":0.4312,"late":0.4223,"near":20,"largestBelow":9960163},{"department":"Municipal Transportation Agency","contracts":343,"agreed":4415389227,"belowCount":313,"be